---
import ComparisonTable from "@components/article/ComparisonTable.astro";
import FAQSection from "@components/article/FAQSection.astro";
import RelatedArticles from "@components/article/RelatedArticles.astro";
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";

// Article metadata
const title = "Managed WordPress Hosting: Who's Actually Worth It (2025)";
const description =
  "I tested five managed WordPress hosts for remote businesses. Here's which ones are worth the premium and which are just overpriced shared hosting honestly.";
const author = "FloatJet Team";
const authorUrl = "/about";
const pubDate = new Date("2025-11-23");
const readingTime = 4;
const image = "/images/unsplash/photo-1504639725590-34d0984388bd-1200w.webp";
const imageAlt = "WordPress development and hosting concept";

// Table of contents
const headings = [
  { text: "Why I Care About This", slug: "introduction", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Quick Comparison", slug: "quick-comparison", depth: 2 },
  { text: "What You Actually Get", slug: "what-is-managed", depth: 2 },
  { text: "The Hosts", slug: "reviews", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Which One?", slug: "how-to-choose", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Quick Answers", slug: "faq", depth: 2 },
  { text: "Bottom Line", slug: "conclusion", depth: 2 },
];

// Comparison table
const comparisonColumns = [
  { key: "host", label: "Host", width: "110px" },
  { key: "price", label: "Starting Price" },
  { key: "sites", label: "Sites" },
  { key: "storage", label: "Storage" },
  { key: "bestFor", label: "Best For" },
];

const comparisonRows = [
  {
    host: "<strong>WP Engine</strong>",
    price: "$20/mo",
    sites: "1",
    storage: "10GB",
    bestFor: "Business sites",
  },
  {
    host: "<strong>Kinsta</strong>",
    price: "$35/mo",
    sites: "1",
    storage: "10GB",
    bestFor: "Performance",
  },
  {
    host: "<strong>Cloudways</strong>",
    price: "$14/mo",
    sites: "Unlimited",
    storage: "25GB",
    bestFor: "Value",
  },
  {
    host: "<strong>Flywheel</strong>",
    price: "$15/mo",
    sites: "1",
    storage: "5GB",
    bestFor: "Designers/Agencies",
  },
  {
    host: "<strong>SiteGround</strong>",
    price: "$15/mo",
    sites: "1",
    storage: "20GB",
    bestFor: "Budget managed",
  },
];

// FAQ data
const faqs = [
  {
    question: "Worth the extra cost?",
    answer: "<p>For business sites, yes. For hobby blogs, probably not.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I use any plugins?",
    answer:
      "<p>Most work. They block caching plugins (they have their own) and some security plugins. WooCommerce, Elementor, SEO stuff—all fine.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "What if traffic exceeds my plan?",
    answer:
      "<p>WP Engine and Kinsta charge overages. Cloudways lets you scale the server up. They'll warn you first.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "Need managed for WooCommerce?",
    answer: "<p>Strongly recommended. Stores need the uptime and resources.</p>",
  },
  {
    question: "How do updates work?",
    answer: "<p>Automatic. They test in staging first. One-click rollback if something breaks.</p>",
  },
];

// Related articles
const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: "Best Web Hosting for Remote Businesses",
    excerpt: "Complete comparison of all hosting types for remote teams.",
    href: "/tools/best-web-hosting-remote-business",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1558494949-ef010cbdcc31-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Server infrastructure",
  },
  {
    title: "Cloud vs Traditional Hosting",
    excerpt: "Understand the differences to choose the right hosting type.",
    href: "/blog/cloud-vs-traditional-hosting",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1451187580459-43490279c0fa-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Cloud computing concept",
  },
  {
    title: "Best Project Management Software",
    excerpt: "Organize your WordPress projects with the right PM tools.",
    href: "/tools/best-project-management-software",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1454165804606-c3d57bc86b40-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Project management dashboard",
  },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    author={author}
    authorUrl={authorUrl}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Hosting"
    categorySlug="tools/hosting"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <h2 id="introduction">Why I Care About This</h2>
  <p>
    My first "real" WordPress site crashed on launch day. <a href="/blog/cloud-vs-traditional-hosting">Shared
    hosting</a>. Traffic spike. White screen.
  </p>
  <p>
    I was refreshing the page every 30 seconds like that would fix it. Support took 4 hours to respond.
    By then, the moment was gone.
  </p>
  <p>
    That site is now on managed hosting. Hasn't gone down in two years.
  </p>
  <p>
    Is managed hosting worth 5x the price of shared? For a hobby blog, probably not. For anything
    <a href="/tools/best-ecommerce-hosting">making money</a> or representing your business? Absolutely.
  </p>

  <!-- Quick Comparison -->
  <h2 id="quick-comparison">Quick Comparison</h2>

  <ComparisonTable columns={comparisonColumns} rows={comparisonRows}/>

  <p>
    <strong>My picks:</strong> WP Engine if reliability is everything. Kinsta if speed is everything.
    Cloudways if you want value.
  </p>

  <!-- What is Managed WordPress Hosting? -->
  <h2 id="what-is-managed">What You Actually Get</h2>
  <p>
    Managed hosting means they handle the WordPress stuff:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Updates happen automatically. Core, plugins, themes.</li>
    <li>Daily backups. One click to restore.</li>
    <li>Security monitoring. They catch problems before you do.</li>
    <li>Staging sites. Test changes without breaking live.</li>
    <li>WordPress-specific support. Not some general tech person reading a script.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <a href="/tools/best-web-hosting-remote-business">Shared hosting</a>? You're on a server with hundreds of other
    sites. Their traffic spike becomes
    your slow site. Support is generic. You're mostly on your own.
  </p>

  <!-- Top Providers Reviewed -->
  <h2 id="reviews">The Hosts</h2>

  <h3 id="wpengine">WP Engine</h3>
  <p>
    The safe choice. Industry standard since 2010. Major brands use them. If something goes wrong
    at 2am, actual <a href="/tools/best-project-management-software">WordPress experts</a> answer.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What I like:</strong> Never had a single downtime issue. Support knows WordPress deeply.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What's annoying:</strong> $20/month for one site feels steep. Traffic limits are strict.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/go/wpengine" rel="nofollow sponsored" class="text-jet-stream hover:text-sky-light">
      Try WP Engine (2 months free) &rarr;
    </a>
  </p>

  <h3 id="kinsta">Kinsta</h3>
  <p>
    Fastest I've tested. Runs on Google Cloud's premium tier. Their dashboard is genuinely nice to use.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What I like:</strong> Speed is noticeably better. Modern interface.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What's annoying:</strong> $35/month. Most expensive option. Worth it if speed matters to your business.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/go/kinsta" rel="nofollow sponsored" class="text-jet-stream hover:text-sky-light">
      Try Kinsta &rarr;
    </a>
  </p>

  <h3 id="cloudways">Cloudways</h3>
  <p>
    Different approach. You pick your cloud (DigitalOcean, AWS, Google) and they manage WordPress
    on it. Unlimited sites per server.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What I like:</strong> Best value by far. $14/month for unlimited sites.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What's annoying:</strong> Slightly more technical. Not as hand-holdy as others.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/go/cloudways" rel="nofollow sponsored" class="text-jet-stream hover:text-sky-light">
      Try Cloudways (3 days free) &rarr;
    </a>
  </p>

  <h3 id="flywheel">Flywheel</h3>
  <p>
    Built for designers and agencies. Client billing transfer, collaboration tools. WP Engine owns them now.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What I like:</strong> Perfect workflow for client work.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What's annoying:</strong> Lower performance tier. 5K visits on starter plan is tight.
  </p>

  <h3 id="siteground">SiteGround</h3>
  <p>
    Budget option that's actually good. Straddles the line between shared and managed.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What I like:</strong> Managed features at almost-shared prices.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What's annoying:</strong> Renewal rates jump. That $3/month intro price becomes $15.
  </p>

  <!-- How to Choose -->
  <h2 id="how-to-choose">Which One?</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Budget:</strong> SiteGround or Cloudways starter</li>
    <li><strong><a href="/guides/productivity">Business site</a>:</strong> WP Engine</li>
    <li><strong>Speed obsessed:</strong> Kinsta</li>
    <li><strong>Multiple sites:</strong> Cloudways (no contest)</li>
    <li><strong>Building for clients:</strong> Flywheel</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Not technical? WP Engine, Kinsta, and Flywheel are the most beginner-friendly. Cloudways has
    more options but expects you to know a bit more.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <!-- Conclusion -->
  <h2 id="conclusion">Bottom Line</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/go/wpengine" rel="nofollow sponsored">WP Engine</a> for reliability.
    <a href="/go/kinsta" rel="nofollow sponsored">Kinsta</a> for speed.
    <a href="/go/cloudways" rel="nofollow sponsored">Cloudways</a> for value.
  </p>
  <p>
    All three migrate your site for free. The upgrade from shared hosting is worth it if your site matters.
  </p>
  <p>
    I learned that the hard way. You don't have to.
  </p>
  <p>
    For more hosting options, see our <a href="/tools/best-web-hosting-remote-business">complete web hosting guide</a>.
  </p>

  <!-- Related Articles -->
  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
